      "current_summary": "Transfer funds",
      "current_description": "Versions 1 and 2 of the Transfers API are deprecated. If you are just starting your implementation, use the latest version.\n\nStarts a request to transfer funds to:\n- [Balance accounts](https://docs.adyen.com/api-explorer/#/balanceplatform/latest/post/balanceAccounts)\n - [Transfer instruments](https://docs.adyen.com/api-explorer/#/legalentity/latest/post/transferInstruments)\n - [Third-party bank accounts](https://docs.adyen.com/payouts/payout-service/pay-out-to-bank-accounts)\n - [Third-party cards](https://docs.adyen.com/payouts/payout-service/pay-out-to-cards)\n \nAdyen sends the outcome of the transfer request through webhooks.\n\nTo use this endpoint:\n- Your API credential must have the **TransferService Webservice Initiate** [role](https://docs.adyen.com/platforms/manage-access/webservice-roles/?tab=transfers_3).\n - The account holder must have the required [capabilities](https://docs.adyen.com/platforms/verification-overview/capabilities).\n \nReach out to your Adyen contact to set up these permissions.",
      "description_suggestion": "Initiates an asynchronous transfer request to move funds from a source balance account to a destination (balance account, transfer instrument, third-party bank account, or card). Requires a TransferInfo request body specifying the source, destination, and amount. Returns a 202 Accepted response with a transfer reference. The transfer outcome is delivered asynchronously via webhooks. Requires the TransferService Webservice Initiate role and appropriate account holder capabilities. Note: This is an asynchronous operation; the 202 response indicates the request was accepted, not that the transfer completed.",
      "reasoning": "Summary is clear and actionable. Description has good context about supported destinations and permission requirements, but lacks clarity on the asynchronous nature of the operation and what the response contains. Added explicit mention of the 202 Accepted status code, clarified that this is asynchronous with webhook delivery, specified the request schema (TransferInfo), and emphasized that 202 means acceptance not completion. This helps AI agents understand they must handle asynchronous callbacks and not treat the response as confirmation of transfer completion.",
